s the target model\u2019s probability distribution for each drafted token and for the next token after the draft.\n\nThe key trick is verification. For each drafted token, the target model compares its own probability for that token with the draft model\u2019s probability. If the target model would have chosen the token with at least as high probability, the token is accepted. If not, the token is rejected, and the target model samples a replacement from a corrected distribution that favors the target model\u2019s preferences while accounting for the draft. This rejection-sampling step is what preserves the exact output distribution of ordinary target-model decoding: every token is ultimately drawn from the target model\u2019s conditional probabilities, not from the draft model. The draft only changes the order in which the target model evaluates positions, not the final probabilities.\n\nIn practice, speedup depends on how many drafted tokens are accepted before a rejection, the relative cost of the draft and target models, and the efficiency of the target model\u2019s parallel verification pass. A high acceptance rate means the target model produces multiple tokens per forward pass, reducing the number of expensive passes needed. The speedup is limited by the draft model\u2019s quality, the target model\u2019s latency, and hardware constraints on batch size and memory.\n\nThe two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are low draft acceptance rates, often because the draft model is too weak or the task is too unpredictable, and high overhead from running the draft model or verifying long", "top2": [[[1596, -0.008125565014779568], [1421, -5.577061176300049]], [[1144, -1.0013630344474223e-05], [513, -11.765589714050293]], [[4087, -0.255711168050766], [310, -1.5351454019546509]], [[1156, -0.0015904430765658617], [303, -6.602426052093506]], [[579, -0.2176932692527771], [25, -2.044015884399414]], [[1622, -0.0006679738871753216], [3296, -8.147098541259766